# This project pins react/react-dom to 19.0.0-rc-66855b96-20241106, which is
# exactly the prerelease next@15.0.3 declares as its peer
# (`^18.2.0 || 19.0.0-rc-66855b96-20241106`).
#
# @m3e/react asks for `react: ">=18"`, and a prerelease never satisfies a range
# that does not itself name one. npm therefore goes looking for a real React to
# satisfy that peer, lands on the newest stable, and reports a conflict against
# the pinned RC -- failing `npm ci` outright with ERESOLVE.
#
# The dependency graph is actually consistent; this is npm's prerelease rule
# inventing a conflict. Resolving it with `--legacy-peer-deps` skips peer
# resolution instead of second-guessing the pin. Stable React 19 is not an
# option here without also moving off next@15.0.3, whose peer range excludes it.
legacy-peer-deps=true